Abstract Lead (Pb) is a widely occurring heavy metal
employed in industrial products and hence released into
the environment, causing several environmental health
risk concerns. This study comprehensively surveyed
the literature on Pb contamination in different agricultural foods and food products commonly consumed by
Bangladeshi inhabitants and assessed associated cancer
and non-cancer health risks. Cereals (i.e., rice, wheat
and maize) contained very high concentrations of Pb
among the selected food items, the highest was found
in wheat (4.04 µg ­g−1), while rice and maize were 2.22
and 1.43 µg ­g−1, respectively, that exceeded the maximum allowable concentration (MAC, 0.20 µg ­g−1) for
